{"eligibility":"exclude","exclusion_criterion":"EC5","side_list":"correctness-only","eligibility_reason":"The paper fine-tunes Llama-3.2-3B to decompile TAC into Solidity, so it is an LLM technique producing code. However, the quantitative outcomes are only edit distance, semantic similarity to the original source, length difference and token frequency, which measure fidelity rather than an NF dimension. Readability claims are qualitative only, and no readability, efficiency or security metric is computed for the generated code.","title":"Decompiling Smart Contracts with a Large Language Model","year":2025,"venue":"preprint","venue_type":"","study_type":"","techniques":[],"compute_reported":[],"contamination_handled":"","human_eval":"","human_eval_details":"","artifact_url":"","reimplementable":"","qa":{},"confidence":"medium","notes":"Borderline case. The paper repeatedly claims 'markedly superior readability' but gives no quantitative readability metric and no quantitative comparison against traditional decompilers. The only comparison is a vague statement that traditional decompilers 'typically achieve' lower scores. Edit distance and semantic similarity to the original source could be read as a readability proxy if the reviewer interprets them that way. The ablation against the base model on 663 functions reports a 45% semantic similarity drop."}
