{"eligibility":"include","exclusion_criterion":"","side_list":"none","eligibility_reason":"The paper proposes an LLM framework (GPT-4 Turbo) that uses RAG, compiler feedback and retrieved optimization strategies to rewrite C/C++ into HLS-C code. It reports quantitative efficiency outcomes for the LLM-edited code: area, power and minimum clock period of the synthesized circuit. Inclusion is borderline because the inputs are human-written programs (EC4/adjacent risk) and efficiency here means hardware PPA rather than software runtime.","title":"Automated C/C++ Program Repair for High-Level Synthesis via Large Language Models","year":2024,"venue":"Workshop on Machine Learning for CAD (MLCAD); arXiv 2407.03889","venue_type":"workshop","study_type":"technique-proposal","techniques":[{"technique_name":"LLM-driven HLS repair framework: RAG repair templates, iterative compiler-feedback repair, LLM-generated bit-width optimization script, and RAG-guided pragma PPA optimization","family_v0":"hybrid","stage":"post-generation-loop","signal_source":["compiler","retrieved-knowledge","profiler","test-execution"],"tools":"Catapult HLS; Synopsys Design Compiler with Nangate 45nm library; Sentence-BERT retriever","iterations":"Up to 5 LLM queries per instance based on HLS compiler errors; repair repeats until compilation succeeds","dims_targeted":["efficiency"],"dims_measured":["efficiency"],"correctness_measured":true,"metrics":"efficiency: circuit area, power and minimum clock period after synthesis [execution/tool-based synthesis]; LLM cost in tokens. Correctness: compilation and C-RTL co-simulation pass rate.","benchmarks":"24 real-world applications collected from prior HLS work and LeetCode, covering 8 incompatibility types","languages":"C/C++ (HLS-C)","models":"GPT-4 Turbo, closed, instruct","baselines":"Traditional scripts [6][7]; direct GPT-4 Turbo repair; GPT-4 Turbo with scripts (no RAG); default 32-bit int implementation; designs before PPA optimization","effect_target":"+","effect_target_headline":"average reduction of 36.57%, 33.03%, and 29.08% in area, power, and minimum clock period (bit width optimization vs 32-bit int); PPA optimization: average reduction of 14.02%, 11.50%, and 17.94%","effect_correctness":"+","effect_correctness_headline":"average 23.33% and 13.89% increase in repair pass rate (vs GPT-4 Turbo baseline and GPT-4 Turbo with scripts)","effect_other_dims":{"readability-maintainability":"NR","efficiency":"targeted","security":"NR"}}],"compute_reported":["tokens","cost"],"contamination_handled":"no","human_eval":"none","human_eval_details":"","artifact_url":"","reimplementable":"partial","qa":{"QA1":0.5,"QA2":0.5,"QA3":1,"QA4":1,"QA5":0,"QA6":0.5,"QA7":1,"QA8":0,"QA9":0,"QA10":0},"confidence":"low","notes":"Eligibility is borderline. The paper is LLM repair/translation of human-written C into HLS-C, so it could be classed as adjacent (EC4). Efficiency is measured as hardware PPA, which may fall outside the review's notion of software efficiency. Efficiency results come from figures only: bit-width optimization on 5 benchmarks and PPA optimization on 6. The PPA comparison has no baseline other than before/after. Pass rates are computed over 15 instances, but no variance is reported. The 21.56% cost reduction reported for joint LLM-script repair is likely a separate technique. Table II is somewhat garbled."}
